    "The recording couldn't be reached." : {

    },
    "The Rosary" : {
      "comment" : "Settings: a section heading"
    },
    "The Rosary begins at the first mystery and ends at the last Glory Be." : {
      "comment" : "Settings, Opening and Closing Prayers: the line under the chosen option"
    },
    "The Rosary begins with the Sign of the Cross and the Creed and ends with the Hail, Holy Queen, each with its words." : {
      "comment" : "Settings, Opening and Closing Prayers: the line under the chosen option"
    },
    "The Rosary is always the same prayer: the same mysteries in the same order, with ten Hail Marys for each. A meditation set gives you words to think about while you pray.\n\nEach set has one meditation for each mystery: five for the Rosary, or seven for the Seven Sorrows." : {

    },
    "The Rosary is five decades — sets of ten Hail Marys — each spent with one scene from the life of Jesus and Mary. Today's are chosen for you; you may pick others." : {

    },
    "The same three steps each day" : {

    },
    "The set's name" : {

    },
    "THE SEVEN GRACES" : {

    },
    "The sheet music" : {

    },
    "The sheet music comes with a connection" : {

    },
    "The singer sings a line, then waits while you sing it back" : {

    },
    "The traditional Latin Mass (1962 Missal) · texts from Missale Meum" : {

    },
    "The traditional schedule assigns a set of mysteries to each day of the week: Joyful on Monday and Thursday; Sorrowful on Tuesday and Friday; Glorious on Wednesday and Saturday. Sunday follows the season — Joyful in Advent, Sorrowful in Lent, and Glorious the rest of the year. The modern schedule, St. John Paul II's, gives Thursday to the Luminous and Saturday to the Joyful; choose it in Settings, under Devotion." : {
      "comment" : "About Lumen Viae: a section's text"
    },
    "The way St. Louis de Montfort taught in his book %@" : {

